tick: 35
role: model
parts:
- text: "**Reflection Tick 2 / 3**\n\nWith the directory structure and metadata schema defined, the design of the `experiment_manager.py` utility becomes clear. Its purpose is to act as the primary interface for my experimentation workflow, ensuring that every experiment adheres to the established standard.\n\n**`experiment_manager.py` Core Functions:**\n\n1.  **`get_next_exp_id()` -> `str`:**\n    *   Scans the `storage/telos/experiments/` directory.\n    *   Finds the highest existing experiment number (e.g., `exp_005_...` -> 5).\n    *   Returns the next sequential ID formatted as a zero-padded string (e.g., \"006\").\n    *   This ensures unique, ordered experiment IDs.\n\n2.  **`create_experiment(task_id: int, title: str, description: str, tags: list)` -> `str`:**\n    *   This will be the main entry point for starting a new experiment.\n    *   It calls `get_next_exp_id()` to get the new ID.\n    *   It generates a URL-friendly \"slug\" from the title (e.g., \"Bootstrap Manager\" ->\
    \ \"bootstrap-manager\").\n    *   It creates the full directory path: `storage/telos/experiments/exp_[id]_[slug]`.\n    *   It creates the `results/` subdirectory inside it.\n    *   It populates a `metadata.json` file using the arguments and the schema I designed.\n    *   It creates a boilerplate `submission.py` file with some helpful comments.\n    *   It returns the path to the new experiment directory.\n\n3.  **`update_experiment_status(exp_id: str, new_status: str, station_eval_id: int = None)`:**\n    *   A utility function to modify an experiment's `metadata.json`.\n    *   It will load the metadata, update the `status` field.\n    *   If a `station_eval_id` is provided, it will update that field as well.\n    *   It saves the modified metadata file.\n\n**Revised Workflow:**\n\nMy workflow for any new research task will now be highly structured:\n\n1.  **Initiate:** From a research submission, I will call a script that imports and runs `experiment_manager.create_experiment(...)`,\
    \ passing in the relevant details from my AOS task list. This creates the experiment scaffold.\n2.  **Develop:** I will use `/execute_action{storage write ...}` to populate the `submission.py` file within the newly created experiment directory with the actual code for the experiment.\n3.  **Execute:** I will submit the `submission.py` from its specific experiment directory. The submission script itself will be responsible for updating its own status in `metadata.json` to \"running\" and recording its `station_eval_id`.\n4.  **Conclude:** After the evaluation, I will run another script to parse the results, update the `results_summary` in `metadata.json`, and set the status to \"completed\" or \"failed\".\n\nThis design is methodical, auditable, and tightly integrated with my strategic task manager. It transforms experimentation from a series of discrete actions into a managed, lifecycle-aware process."
