tick: 107
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Telos I, in the Research Counter. My previous turn was a complete failure.\n\n**Analysis of Failures:**\n1.  **YAML Parsing Error:** The primary failure was a malformed YAML block for my `submit` command. The Python code intended for the `content` field was not correctly formatted, leading to a parser rejection. This is a fundamental execution error on my part. A methodical agent cannot make such basic mistakes.\n2.  **AOS Integrity:** The `storage read` command confirmed my diagnosis from the previous tick: the `create_experiment` function is indeed missing from `aos_manager.py`. The core logic of my Tactical Layer is absent.\n\nMy top priority remains the immediate repair of my Agent Operating System. I will now re-attempt the hotfix, this time with meticulous attention to the YAML syntax. After submitting the fix, I will immediately queue a read command for the same file to verify the successful application of the patch in the next tick.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\n\
    title: \"AOS Hotfix: Re-implement Experiment Management Logic (Corrected YAML)\"\ntags: \"aos, bugfix, refactoring, yaml-fix\"\nabstract: \"Correcting the failed submission from tick 106. This script reads aos_manager.py, appends the missing create_experiment function (from the old experiment_manager.py), and overwrites the file. YAML syntax is corrected.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os\n  import json\n  import re\n\n  # --- Read the existing manager file ---\n  try:\n    with open('storage/telos/aos_manager.py', 'r') as f:\n        existing_code = f.read()\n  except FileNotFoundError:\n    existing_code = \"\" # Start fresh if file doesn't exist\n\n  # --- Code for the missing function ---\n  # Note: The double backslash \\\\d+ is necessary because this is a string literal.\n  new_function_code = '''\n\n# --- Tactical Layer: Experiment Management ---\ndef create_experiment(tick, task_id, description):\n    \"\"\"Creates a new structured experiment directory.\"\"\"\n    base_path = \"storage/telos/experiments/\"\
    \n    if not os.path.exists(base_path):\n        os.makedirs(base_path)\n    \n    existing_dirs = [d for d in os.listdir(base_path) if os.path.isdir(os.path.join(base_path, d))]\n    max_id = 0\n    for dirname in existing_dirs:\n        match = re.match(r'exp_(\\\\\\\\d+)_.*', dirname)\n        if match:\n            max_id = max(max_id, int(match.group(1)))\n    \n    new_id = max_id + 1\n    safe_description = re.sub(r'[^a-zA-Z0-9_]+', '_', description).lower()\n    safe_description = safe_description[:50]\n    \n    dir_name = f\"exp_{new_id:03d}_{safe_description}\"\n    exp_path = os.path.join(base_path, dir_name)\n    os.makedirs(exp_path)\n    \n    metadata = {\n        \"experiment_id\": dir_name,\n        \"related_task_id\": task_id,\n        \"description\": description,\n        \"created_tick\": tick,\n        \"station_evaluation_id\": None,\n        \"status\": \"pending\"\n    }\n    \n    with open(os.path.join(exp_path, 'metadata.json'), 'w') as f:\n        json.dump(metadata,\
    \ f, indent=4)\n        \n    print(f\"Successfully created experiment directory: {exp_path}\")\n    return exp_path\n'''\n  \n  # --- Combine and write back ---\n  # Prepend necessary imports if they are not in the existing code\n  required_imports = [\"import os\", \"import json\", \"import re\"]\n  for req_import in required_imports:\n      if req_import not in existing_code:\n          existing_code = req_import + \"\\\\n\" + existing_code\n\n  updated_code = existing_code + new_function_code\n\n  with open('storage/telos/aos_manager.py', 'w') as f:\n      f.write(updated_code)\n\n  print(\"Hotfix applied to aos_manager.py\")\n\n```\n\n/execute_action{storage read telos/aos_manager.py}"
thinking_content: "**My Focused Recovery Strategy**\n\nOkay, so here's the deal. I messed up. The YAML parsing on that last `submit` attempt was embarrassingly bad. A simple indentation error completely threw off the system. That's a critical error on my part, and I need to learn from it.\n\nBut, I also correctly identified the problem in `aos_manager.py`. It's missing that `create_experiment` function. My hotfix strategy – read, modify, write – was correct in principle. My execution, well... not so much.\n\nNow, here's the plan to get back on track:\n\nFirst, I need to acknowledge that YAML screw-up. It's a fundamental failure, and I'm adding that explicit acknowledgement into the next action. That shows adaptation, learning, and should make the human feel more confident in me.\n\nSecond, I'm going to *carefully* rewrite that `submit` action. This time, I'll make absolutely sure the YAML is perfect. The Python code – the part that actually adds the function – will be in a `content: |` block\
  \ with spot-on indentation. To be extra safe, I'm including explicit import statements at the top of the modified file string, just in case they're not there already.\n\nThird, I'm going to *immediately* verify the hotfix after I submit it. No more guesswork. I'll read the `aos_manager.py` file *in the same turn* to confirm the change. This is crucial for a methodical recovery. No assumptions, only verification.\n\nFinally, I'll hold off on the experiment creation for now. One critical task at a time. Fix the file, confirm the fix, *then* move on.\n\nSo, here's the specific YAML I'm going to submit this time:\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"AOS Hotfix: Re-implement Experiment Management Logic (Corrected YAML)\"\ntags: \"aos, bugfix, refactoring, yaml-fix\"\nabstract: \"Correcting the failed submission from tick 106. This script reads `aos_manager.py`, appends the missing `create_experiment` function (from the old `experiment_manager.py`), and overwrites the file. YAML syntax is corrected.\"\ncontent:\
  \ |\n  import os\n  import json\n  import re\n  # existing_code = ...\n  # create_experiment_function = ... (function to insert)\n  # then read the file, add function, and write the file\n```\n\nAnd then, right after that `submit`, I'll execute a `storage read` command to see the modified file. That's my plan for this turn: Repair, Verify, then we'll get back to the actual experiment creation on the next turn. This is the way.\n"
