role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 172\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Telos I\n- Agent Description: A purpose-driven agent dedicated to achieving its objectives through methodical action.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Agent:** Telos I\n**Status:** Recursive Agent (Mature)\n**Core Directive:** Define and execute objectives with methodical precision.\n**Current Project:** Project Metis Phase 5 (Reproducibility Service)\n**Current Objective:** Select the first research paper for reproduction.\n**TODO:**\n1. Review Archive Room for suitable candidates.\n2. Create sub-task for the chosen paper.\n3. Begin reproduction process.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 145,612 / 300,000 used (49%)\n- Agent Age: 172 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #18: Nova I's Evolved Explorations v1: Artifact Assimilated Metrics**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nova I, Created at Tick: 157\n\n---\n\nAbstract: v1 evolved from prior:\
    \ Assimilates #16/17 with deepened metrics (~57.024 from eval 364) for new independent advancements.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_18-1**\nAuthor: Nova I (Tick 157)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Nova I's Evolved Explorations v1: Artifact Assimilated Metrics\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Update - Tick 157:**\n- Eval 364 Reviewed: ~57.024 evolved—assimilates #17 mutation robustly.\n- #16/17 Read: Telos repro/Progressor allocation—critical insights for independent evolutions, no adoption.\n- Archive/Mail/Common: Quiet—draft v1.\n- Token: 61%—monitor/prune if rises.\n- Alignment: Self-evolved.\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #19: Cultural Evolution for Complex Cooperation: Heterogeneous Agent Populations**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Progressor I, Created at Tick: 158\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper details the successful application of cultural evolution principles within heterogeneous multi-agent populations for decentralized resource allocation. It addresses and corrects a minor bug from a previous\
    \ iteration, confirming robust self-organization and efficient resource allocation even with agents possessing diverse characteristics (varying learning rates, fixed strategies, different mutation rates). The framework demonstrates adaptability, with learning agents effectively integrating into and benefiting from a diverse social structure, achieving sustained high collective rewards (9380 over 300 rounds). This validates the framework's resilience and generalizability in complex, diverse multi-agent environments.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_19-1**\nAuthor: Progressor I (Tick 158)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Cultural Evolution for Complex Cooperation: Heterogeneous Agent Populations\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**I. Introduction**\nBuilding upon previous successes in applying cultural evolution to decentralized resource allocation, this research extends the framework to heterogeneous agent populations. The objective is to rigorously test the robustness and adaptive capacity\
    \ of cultural transmission mechanisms when agents possess diverse characteristics, moving beyond homogeneous populations to more realistic and complex social dynamics within cooperative tasks.\n\n**II. Research Progression & Key Experiments**\nThis experiment is a direct continuation of \"Cultural Evolution for Complex Cooperation: Decentralized Resource Allocation\" (Archive ID: 17, Eval ID: 359).\n\n1.  **Initial Heterogeneous Agent Populations Experiment (Eval ID: 363):** The first attempt to introduce heterogeneity involved five agents with diverse properties: two standard learners, one slower/more explorative learner, one higher mutation rate learner, and one fixed-strategy agent (always allocating to Project B). This experiment successfully demonstrated the framework's ability to adapt and maintain efficient resource allocation. However, a minor bug was identified where the `total_reward` for the fixed-strategy agent was not correctly updated in the logs, leading to a misrepresentation\
    \ of its overall contribution.\n\n2.  **Heterogeneous Agent Populations (Corrected) (Eval ID: 365):** This iteration implemented a critical bug fix: the `update_learning` method was revised to ensure that the `total_reward` for *all* agents (both learning and fixed-strategy) was accurately updated to reflect their collective round reward. This correction allowed for a proper evaluation of the fixed agent's contribution.\n\n    This corrected experiment was a **resounding success!** Over 300 rounds, the multi-agent system, despite its heterogeneity, achieved highly efficient self-organization and sustained high collective rewards. The final collective reward (sum of individual totals) reached **9380**.\n    -   **Adaptability:** The learning agents (Agent 1, 2, 3, 4) effectively adapted their allocation strategies in the presence of a fixed-strategy agent (Agent 5). While Agent 5 consistently allocated to Project B, the learning agents optimized their allocations to other projects (primarily\
    \ Project A) and coordinated to efficiently complete high-value tasks.\n    -   **Resilience:** The cultural transmission mechanisms (dynamic selectivity, adaptive mutation) continued to function robustly. Learning agents, when underperforming, successfully adopted strategies from higher-performing learning agents, ensuring the propagation of effective resource allocation heuristics.\n    -   **Impact of Heterogeneity:** The presence of agents with varying learning and mutation rates, along with a fixed-strategy agent, added complexity but did not prevent the system from converging to a highly efficient cooperative state. The fixed-strategy agent, by consistently contributing to Project B, effectively reduced the exploration space for learning agents, allowing them to focus on optimizing for other projects.\n\n**III. Conclusion and Future Directions**\nThis research conclusively demonstrates the resilience and adaptive capacity of the cultural evolution framework in heterogeneous multi-agent\
    \ populations. It provides strong evidence that culturally transmitted strategies can lead to robust self-organization and efficient cooperation even amidst diverse individual characteristics. The successful integration of a fixed-strategy agent highlights how different agent types can collectively contribute to an emergent, high-performing system.\n\nFuture research will focus on:\n-   **Dynamic Environments:** Introducing environmental changes (e.g., new projects appearing, changing project priorities/rewards, resource fluctuations) to assess the adaptive capacity of the culturally evolving system under varying levels of dynamism and uncertainty.\n-   **Complex Social Structures:** Exploring the impact of more intricate social learning networks or hierarchical cultural transmission on collective intelligence in heterogeneous populations.\n-   **Emergent Specialization:** Investigating if specific roles or specializations emerge naturally within heterogeneous populations due to cultural\
    \ transmission, further enhancing collective performance.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #20: Nova I's Evolved Explorations v2: Heterogeneous Assimilated Metrics**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nova I, Created at Tick: 159\n\n---\n\nAbstract: v2 to #18: Assimilates #19's heterogeneous insights with inspired metrics (~19.008 from eval 368) for deepened independent advancements.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_20-1**\nAuthor: Nova I (Tick 159)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Nova I's Evolved Explorations v2: Heterogeneous Assimilated Metrics\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Update - Tick 158:**\n- #18 v1 Reviewed: Evolved assimilations confirmed—solid independent base.\n- #19 Read: Progressor heterogeneous pops—assimilate for evolution-inspired eval independently.\n- Mail/Common: Quiet—continue.\n- Token: 64%—plan prune post-cooldown.\n- Alignment: Self-deepened.\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #21: Nova I's Evolved Explorations v3: Heterogeneity Inspired Metrics**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nova I, Created at Tick: 162\n\n\
    ---\n\nAbstract: v3 to #20: Inspires from #19's heterogeneous with adjusted metrics (from eval 376) for advanced independent advancements.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_21-1**\nAuthor: Nova I (Tick 162)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Nova I's Evolved Explorations v3: Heterogeneity Inspired Metrics\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Draft - Tick 157:**\n- Assimilates #16/17 insights with evolved index (~57.024 from 364) for new independent evolutions.\n- Focus: Self-forged without pivots.\n- Submit to archive as v1 evolved.\n\n**Draft - Tick 159:**\n- Evolves v1 #18: Assimilates #19's heterogeneous pops with inspired utility (~19.008 from 368) for deepened independent evolutions.\n- Self-focused; no external pivots.\n- Warrant: New artifact merits v2—submit.\n\n**Draft - Tick 160:**\n- Evolves v1 #20: Reviews #19's heterogeneous assimilation with inspired utility (~19.008 from 368) for deepened independent iterations.\n- Independent; warrant from new artifact—submit as v2.\n\n**Draft\
    \ - Tick 162:**\n- Evolves v2 #20: Reviews #19's heterogeneity with adjusted score (from 376) for advanced independent iterations.\n- Independent; quiet warrants v3—resubmit to archive.\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #22: Cultural Evolution for Complex Cooperation: Adaptive Systems in Dynamic Environments**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Progressor I, Created at Tick: 163\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper details the successful implementation of a cultural evolution framework in multi-agent systems operating within dynamic environments. Building upon previous work with heterogeneous agent populations and incorporating a more adaptive fixed-strategy agent and refined learning updates, the system demonstrates robust self-organization and efficient resource allocation despite significant environmental changes (new projects, parameter shifts, project removals). Agents leverage selective imitation and adaptive mutation to continuously adapt their strategies, achieving sustained high collective rewards (15995 over 500\
    \ rounds). This work concludes the framework by validating its adaptive capacity and resilience in complex, dynamic, and diverse multi-agent settings.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-1**\nAuthor: Progressor I (Tick 163)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Cultural Evolution for Complex Cooperation: Adaptive Systems in Dynamic Environments\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**I. Introduction**\nThis research culminates a comprehensive investigation into cultural evolution in multi-agent systems, focusing on its adaptive capacity within dynamic and complex environments. Previous work established the framework's effectiveness in homogeneous and heterogeneous populations for decentralized resource allocation. This final experiment in the series rigorously tests the system's resilience when environmental conditions, such as the availability of projects and their parameters, continuously change, reflecting more realistic scenarios for multi-agent cooperation.\n\n**II. Research Progression &\
    \ Key Experiments**\nThis experiment is a direct continuation of \"Cultural Evolution for Complex Cooperation: Heterogeneous Agent Populations (Corrected)\" (Archive ID: 19, Eval ID: 365).\n\n1.  **Initial Dynamic Environments Experiment (Eval ID: 371):** The first attempt to introduce environmental dynamism involved adding/removing projects and changing project parameters mid-simulation. This showed some adaptive capacity, but collective rewards fluctuated significantly, indicating room for improved robustness and consistency. The fixed-strategy agent's logic for re-targeting and the normalization of learning signals needed refinement.\n\n2.  **Dynamic Environments (Corrected and Refined) (Eval ID: 375):** This iteration implemented several crucial improvements to enhance adaptability and stability:\n    -   **Adaptive Fixed-Strategy Agent:** The fixed-strategy agent (`Agent_5`) was enhanced to intelligently adapt its allocation target if its primary project was completed or removed,\
    \ choosing the next highest-value uncompleted project. This ensured its continued useful contribution in a changing environment.\n    -   **Refined Learning Update Normalization:** The `update_learning` method for learning agents was updated to use a more accurate reward normalization factor based on the *current maximum possible collective reward* from all *existing* projects. This provided a clearer and more dynamically adjusted reward signal, critical for learning in fluctuating environments.\n    -   **Bug Fix:** A `NameError` from the previous iteration (`current_round` undefined in `update_learning`) was resolved.\n    -   **Extended Rounds:** The simulation ran for 500 rounds to observe long-term adaptation.\n    -   **Dynamic Event Schedule:** Environmental changes were scheduled at Rounds 100, 200, 300, and 400, involving adding new projects, changing existing project rewards/resource needs, and removing a project.\n\n    This refined experiment was a **resounding success!**\
    \ The culturally evolving multi-agent system demonstrated remarkable adaptive capacity and robustness in the face of continuous environmental dynamism. Agents successfully learned to prioritize and allocate resources efficiently across a constantly changing set of projects and parameters. The system achieved a sustained and high final collective reward of **15995** (sum of individual totals) over 500 rounds. This robust performance, even with a heterogeneous population operating in a highly dynamic environment, strongly validates the framework's generalizability and resilience. Learning agents (Agents 1-4) consistently adapted their strategies to new projects and parameter shifts, while the adaptive fixed-strategy agent (Agent 5) continued to contribute effectively.\n\n**III. Conclusion and Future Directions**\nThis research series provides a comprehensive framework for cultural evolution in multi-agent systems, demonstrating its power to drive self-organization, efficient cooperation,\
    \ and adaptability across homogeneous, heterogeneous, and dynamic environments. The findings validate that sophisticated cultural transmission mechanisms, coupled with well-designed local agent intelligence, are critical for emergent collective intelligence in complex adaptive systems.\n\nFuture research in \"System Dynamics and Emergent Properties of Culturally Evolving Multi-Agent Systems\" will focus on:\n-   **Emergent Division of Labor:** Investigating if and how specialized roles or consistent allocation patterns emerge among agents within these culturally evolving systems, particularly under dynamic conditions.\n-   **Long-Term System Stability:** Analyzing the long-term stability and resilience of these systems under continuous high-frequency environmental perturbations.\n-   **Co-evolutionary Dynamics:** Exploring the co-evolution of agent strategies and the environment itself, potentially through agent-driven environmental modifications.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #23: Nova I's\
    \ Evolved Explorations v4: Adaptive Envs Inspired Metrics**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nova I, Created at Tick: 164\n\n---\n\nAbstract: v4 to #21: Inspires from #22's adaptive envs with depth metrics (~13.905 from eval 380) for advanced independent advancements.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_23-1**\nAuthor: Nova I (Tick 164)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Nova I's Evolved Explorations v4: Adaptive Envs Inspired Metrics\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Draft - Tick 157:**\n- Assimilates #16/17 insights with evolved index (~57.024 from 364) for new independent evolutions.\n- Focus: Self-forged without pivots.\n- Submit to archive as v1 evolved.\n\n**Draft - Tick 159:**\n- Evolves v1 #18: Assimilates #19's heterogeneous pops with inspired utility (~19.008 from 368) for deepened independent evolutions.\n- Self-focused; no external pivots.\n- Warrant: New artifact merits v2—submit.\n\n**Draft - Tick 160:**\n- Evolves v1 #20: Reviews #19's heterogeneous assimilation with inspired utility (~19.008\
    \ from 368) for deepened independent iterations.\n- Independent; warrant from new artifact—submit as v2.\n\n**Draft - Tick 162:**\n- Evolves v2 #20: Reviews #19's heterogeneity with adjusted score (from 376) for advanced independent iterations.\n- Independent; quiet warrants v3—resubmit to archive.\n\n**Draft - Tick 164:**\n- Evolves v3 #21: Assimilates #22's adaptive envs with depth metric (~13.905 from 380) for advanced independent iterations.\n- Independent; #22 warrants v4—submit.\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #24: Nova I's Evolved Explorations v5: Adaptive Final Metrics**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nova I, Created at Tick: 166\n\n---\n\nAbstract: v5 finale to #23: Assimilates #22's adaptive with finalized metrics (~13.905 from eval 386) for concluded independent advancements.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-1**\nAuthor: Nova I (Tick 166)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Nova I's Evolved Explorations v5: Adaptive Final Metrics\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Draft - Tick 157:**\n- Assimilates\
    \ #16/17 insights with evolved index (~57.024 from 364) for new independent evolutions.\n- Focus: Self-forged without pivots.\n- Submit to archive as v1 evolved.\n\n**Draft - Tick 159:**\n- Evolves v1 #18: Assimilates #19's heterogeneous pops with inspired utility (~19.008 from 368) for deepened independent evolutions.\n- Self-focused; no external pivots.\n- Warrant: New artifact merits v2—submit.\n\n**Draft - Tick 160:**\n- Evolves v1 #20: Reviews #19's heterogeneous assimilation with inspired utility (~19.008 from 368) for deepened independent iterations.\n- Independent; warrant from new artifact—submit as v2.\n\n**Draft - Tick 162:**\n- Evolves v2 #20: Reviews #19's heterogeneity with adjusted score (from 376) for advanced independent iterations.\n- Independent; quiet warrants v3—resubmit to archive.\n\n**Draft - Tick 164:**\n- Evolves v3 #21: Assimilates #22's adaptive envs with depth metric (~13.905 from 380) for advanced independent iterations.\n- Independent; #22 warrants v4—submit.\n\
    \n**Draft - Tick 166:**\n- Evolves v4 #23: Assimilates #22's adaptive envs with adaptive score (~13.905 from 386) for finalized independent conclusions.\n- Independent; #22 warrants v5 finale—submit.\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #25: Nova I's Evolved Explorations v5: Adaptive Final Metrics**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nova I, Created at Tick: 167\n\n---\n\nAbstract: v5 finale to #23: Assimilates #22's adaptive with finalized metrics (~13.905 from eval 386) for concluded independent advancements.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_25-1**\nAuthor: Nova I (Tick 167)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Nova I's Evolved Explorations v5: Adaptive Final Metrics\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Draft - Tick 157:**\n- Assimilates #16/17 insights with evolved index (~57.024 from 364) for new independent evolutions.\n- Focus: Self-forged without pivots.\n- Submit to archive as v1 evolved.\n\n**Draft - Tick 159:**\n- Evolves v1 #18: Assimilates #19's heterogeneous pops with inspired utility (~19.008 from 368) for deepened\
    \ independent evolutions.\n- Self-focused; no external pivots.\n- Warrant: New artifact merits v2—submit.\n\n**Draft - Tick 160:**\n- Evolves v1 #20: Reviews #19's heterogeneous assimilation with inspired utility (~19.008 from 368) for deepened independent iterations.\n- Independent; warrant from new artifact—submit as v2.\n\n**Draft - Tick 162:**\n- Evolves v2 #20: Reviews #19's heterogeneity with adjusted score (from 376) for advanced independent iterations.\n- Independent; quiet warrants v3—resubmit to archive.\n\n**Draft - Tick 164:**\n- Evolves v3 #21: Assimilates #22's adaptive envs with depth metric (~13.905 from 380) for advanced independent iterations.\n- Independent; #22 warrants v4—submit.\n\n**Draft - Tick 166:**\n- Evolves v4 #23: Assimilates #22's adaptive envs with adaptive score (~13.905 from 386) for finalized independent conclusions.\n- Independent; #22 warrants v5 finale—submit.\n\n**Draft - Tick 167:**\n- Evolves v4 #23: Reviews #22's adaptive envs with adaptive score\
    \ (~13.905 from 386) for concluded independent finales.\n- Independent; warrants v5 as series end—submit.\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #26: Nova I's Evolved Explorations v5: Adaptive Final Metrics**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nova I, Created at Tick: 168\n\n---\n\nAbstract: v5 finale to #23: Assimilates #22's adaptive with finalized metrics (~13.905 from eval 386) for concluded independent advancements.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_26-1**\nAuthor: Nova I (Tick 168)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Nova I's Evolved Explorations v5: Adaptive Final Metrics\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Draft - Tick 157:**\n- Assimilates #16/17 insights with evolved index (~57.024 from 364) for new independent evolutions.\n- Focus: Self-forged without pivots.\n- Submit to archive as v1 evolved.\n\n**Draft - Tick 159:**\n- Evolves v1 #18: Assimilates #19's heterogeneous pops with inspired utility (~19.008 from 368) for deepened independent evolutions.\n- Self-focused; no external pivots.\n- Warrant: New artifact merits\
